(*
A flat chain of `1+1+1+...+1` used as a CONST INITIALIZER crashes the
compiler even though the identical chain in an ordinary statement does not
(see deeply-chained-addition-does-not-crash-the-compiler.pas next door,
which is exactly that statement-context case).  Sema::checkBlock's Phase 2 (Sema.cpp)
tests every const value for a forward reference to an enum literal declared
later in the same block (EP lets const and type sections interleave in any
order) through a lambda, refsPendingEnum, that walks the value with
walkExprs (SemaUtil.h) BEFORE checkExpr ever sees it -- unlike the
array-bound case (issue #204, next door as
a-deeply-chained-array-bound-does-not-crash-the-compiler.pas), where
checkExpr always runs on the same expression FIRST and already reports
"nested too deeply" before a second, unguarded walk is ever reached.  Here
walkExprs's own recursion is the first thing to touch a pathologically deep
chain at all, so it is the one whose stack frames ran out -- confirmed with
gdb (follow-fork-mode child, since the driver execs the actual compile as a
child process): 30-something frames of plang::walkExprs calling itself,
rooted in checkBlock's refsPendingEnum lambda, nothing else on the stack.

The fix gives walkStmts/walkExprs (SemaUtil.h) their own recursion ceiling,
sized the same as Sema::MaxExprDepth (Sema.h) and checked the same way:
before Depth is bumped, so a caller already sitting at the ceiling returns
without recursing once more.  It declines silently rather than reporting a
diagnostic of its own, the same way constBound/buildExtentForm do for the
array-bound case above (issue #204): checkExpr runs on this exact const
value moments later regardless of what refsPendingEnum found -- immediately,
via defineConst, if it found no pending reference (this file's case), or
after Phase 3 resolves the block's types, via the deferred StructuredConsts
list, if it did -- and checkExpr is what actually reports "nested too
deeply", reliably, because walkExprs's own ceiling stops its walk
comfortably before either walk could exhaust the real stack.  Checked in
verbatim rather than generated at build time, matching
deeply-chained-addition-does-not-crash-the-compiler.pas's own precedent --
lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is deterministic; nobody
should have to look at 100000 terms to read this file, so the lit
directives come first.

%plang execs the actual compile as a child process (Driver.cpp), so a crash
in it does not turn %plang's own exit into a raw SIGSEGV: the driver
notices its child died by signal (ExecuteAndWait returning negative),
reports THAT as diag::err_program_crashed ("'.../plang' crashed:
Segmentation fault ..."), and still exits 1 either way, crash or clean
diagnostic -- confirmed by running both pre- and post-fix builds directly
rather than assumed.  So `not`'s own exit-code inversion cannot be what
tells the two apart here; FileCheck's text match is: it finds "nested too
deeply" in stderr only once the compiler actually got as far as reporting
it, and a crash's stderr says "crashed" instead, never that phrase, so
FileCheck (and the RUN line as a whole) fails on a real crash exactly as it
should.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: nested too deeply
*)

program t;
const c = ;
begin end.
